/* ==============================================================
   05 — Payload and torque limits
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   SetPayload, SetTorqueLimits, SetTorqueLimitsCfg
   NEEDS     Robot activated. Read before running.
   WATCH     Two features that mostly show up as numbers, not
             as motion
   TIME      ~25 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   1. TELLING THE ROBOT WHAT IT IS CARRYING

       SetPayload(m, cx, cy, cz)

     m           mass in kg, including the tool AND the part
     cx, cy, cz  centre of mass, in mm, in the FRF

   This is optional, and skipping it is one of the most common
   reasons a customer's accuracy is worse than the datasheet.

   The robot computes the motor torques it expects to need. Those
   predictions are only as good as its model of what is hanging
   off the shaft. Tell it the truth and you get:

     - better path tracking, because the controller compensates
       for the load rather than reacting to it
     - meaningful torque limits, since the expected torque is now
       computed against reality

   On a SCARA the payload shows up in two different ways. Its
   WEIGHT is carried by one joint only: joint 3, the shaft, which
   is the only joint working against gravity. Its INERTIA is felt
   by joints 1, 2 and 4 every time the arm swings or the shaft
   turns — and a centre of mass off the shaft axis makes every
   turn of joint 4 swing it round like a weight on a string.

   The mass must include everything on the shaft — tool, adapter,
   fittings, and the workpiece. Which means it changes DURING a
   cycle: heavier when holding a part, lighter after placing it.
   In a demanding application, set it twice per cycle. It costs
   one line. The MCS500's rated payload is 0.5 kg, all of that
   included.

   And the sign rule again: the tool hangs below the end of the
   shaft, so cz is NEGATIVE.

   Default is 0 kg, reapplied on every activation.

   2. TORQUE LIMITS

       SetTorqueLimits(τ1, τ2, τ3, τ4)

   A threshold per joint, as a percentage of that motor's maximum
   allowable torque. All 100% by default.

       SetTorqueLimitsCfg(s, m)

     s — severity
         0  no action
         1  torque status event [3028]
         2  pause motion, plus the event
         4  event plus a torque limit error [3029]

     m — detection mode
         0  trigger if any actual motor torque exceeds its limit
         1  same as 0, but ignore acceleration and deceleration
            periods
         2  trigger if actual torque DEVIATES from the predicted
            torque by more than the limit

   Mode 2 — the default since firmware 11.1 — is the interesting
   one. It compares measured torque against the model's
   prediction, so the threshold is about the UNEXPECTED part of
   the load, not the total. That is what detects "something is in
   the way", and it is why SetPayload matters: a wrong payload
   makes the prediction wrong and the detection meaningless.

   In mode 2 the numbers are small. The manual suggests values
   around 10%, and warns that the prediction itself is usually
   within 10% but can occasionally be off by 20%. So start a
   little above that, run your real trajectory, and tighten.

   Mode 1 exists because acceleration produces large legitimate
   torque peaks. It is mainly useful for joint-space moves —
   during Cartesian moves the rotary joints are almost always
   accelerating or decelerating, so there is little left to
   check.

   With mode 0 you must use either very low accelerations or very
   high torque limits, or you will trip on ordinary motion.

   ON A SCARA, WATCH JOINT 3

   The classic guarded move on this robot is a vertical one: the
   shaft coming down onto a part. If the tool meets something on
   the way down, the shaft is the joint pushing into it, so
   joint 3's torque is where the contact shows. Whatever else you
   set, that is the threshold that does the work.

   HOW TO USE IT HONESTLY

   The manual's own guidance: rely on this only in the vicinity
   of obstacles — for example while applying an adhesive — and
   remember that high accelerations or large movements produce
   high torque peaks. It is not a general-purpose crash sensor
   bolted over a whole program, and it is NOT a certified safety
   function. It is a process guard for the few millimetres where
   contact is plausible.

   Note also that SetTorqueLimits is a motion command: it goes in
   the queue and takes effect at its position in the sequence,
   not immediately. That is exactly what you want — tighten the
   limits just before the delicate move and release them after.

   Torque limits can only be applied once the robot is activated.

   ------------------------------------------------------------
   CAUTION

   The block below deliberately sets tight torque limits. On a
   robot with a real payload this may trip during ordinary
   motion. Start with the values here on a bare robot, and raise
   them if you get spurious [3029] errors.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)
SetCartLinVel(60)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* --- Declaring the payload -------------------------------------
   A small tool on the shaft: say 0.1 kg, its centre of mass
   15 mm below the end of the shaft, on the axis. Replace with
   your real numbers. */

SetPayload(0.1, 0, 0, -15)

MovePose(170, 0, 100, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Payload changes during the cycle --------------------------
   Holding a 0.2 kg part triples the mass and moves the centre of
   mass further down. Declaring it is one line, and it is the
   difference between the datasheet accuracy and something worse. */

MoveLin(170, 0, 60, 0)
Delay(0.5)
// (grip the part here)

SetPayload(0.3, 0, 0, -30)             // tool + part

MoveLinRelWrf(0, 0, 40, 0)
MovePose(170, 60, 100, 0)
MoveLin(170, 60, 60, 0)
Delay(0.5)
// (release the part here)

SetPayload(0.1, 0, 0, -15)             // tool alone again

MoveLinRelWrf(0, 0, 40, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Torque limits around one delicate move --------------------
   The pattern to actually use: tighten just before the move that
   might touch something, release immediately after. */

// Report and error if torque deviates from prediction.
SetTorqueLimitsCfg(4, 2)

MovePose(170, 0, 100, 0)

// Tighten, for the descent only. 25% deviation is a cautious
// starting point above the manual's "about 10"; tune it down on
// your own trajectory.
SetTorqueLimits(25, 25, 25, 25)
SetCartLinVel(15)                      // slow, because contact is plausible

MoveLin(170, 0, 60, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelWrf(0, 0, 40, 0)

// Release for ordinary travel.
SetTorqueLimits(100, 100, 100, 100)
SetCartLinVel(150)


// --- Back to defaults and home --------------------------------
// The firmware defaults are 0 kg payload, 100% torque limits,
// and severity 0 with mode 2 — severity 0 meaning a trip takes
// no action. Note there is no "off" detection mode; severity is
// what decides whether a trip does anything.
SetPayload(0, 0, 0, 0)
SetTorqueLimits(100, 100, 100, 100)
SetTorqueLimitsCfg(0, 2)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. SetPayload is the easiest accuracy win there is and it is
      routinely skipped. Ask whether they declare payload on
      their current robots. Often the honest answer is no, and
      you have just explained some of their accuracy complaints.

   2. The tighten-then-release pattern is the one to show an
      integrator doing assembly or dispensing. It is specific,
      it is bounded, and it does not pretend to be more than it
      is.

   3. Be precise about what this is not. Torque limiting is a
      process guard, not a safety rating. Overselling it is the
      fastest way to lose a technical buyer.

   NEXT   06_time_based_motion.mxprog
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
